  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nConsiderable instability has developed across the region this \nafternoon with ML CAPE values of 2500-3000 J/kg. An MCS is \ntaking shape over lower Michigan and is going to be the driving \nfeature for severe thunderstorms to affect the area this \nevening. A Severe Thunderstorm Watch has been issued for the \nentire area through 10 PM and a Flood Watch has been issued for \nportions of Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania through \nMidnight. \n\nThe MCS is moving southeast around the periphery of the upper level \nridge. A bullseye of 40-50 knots of effective shear is forecast to \nmove from Michigan across Lake Erie towards Cleveland and the \nnortheast lakeshore. Damaging wind gusts to 70 mph will be the \nprimary hazard this evening, although isolated stronger wind\ngusts are possible, especially with bowing line segments or cold\npool acceleration. Large hail and a tornado or two are also \npossible given the strong shear and lift in the column. Latest \nCAMs do indicate more of a linear feature that looks fairly \nprogressive through the evening. In this case, the potential \nfor training storms may be somewhat lower. Portions of Northeast\nOhio received up to 2 inches of rain this morning while \nportions of Northwest PA and especially Erie County has received\n2-5 inches of rain over the last 36 hours. This recent rainfall\nwill lead to wet antecedent conditions and flooding or flash \nflooding will be faster to develop. \n\nThe southward extent of storms is less certain, especially south of \nRoute 30 in NW Ohio. If storms can extend farther south, DCAPE \nvalues are very high in the 1200-1400 J/kg range, making downbursts \nvery concerning.  \n\nThis activity will push southeast of the area by 10 PM but a \nboundary will remain from lower Michigan towards Central Ohio. \nAdditional shortwave energy is expected to round the ridge overnight \nand showers could continue along with scattered thunderstorms. Any \nthunderstorms late tonight should pose a lower threat of severe \nweather. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe timing and coverage of storms this evening and late tonight\nwill have an impact on severe weather potential on Friday. One \nshortwave could pass early Friday morning with a second through \nmidday on Friday. Some models indicate that southern portions of\nthe area could destabilize again between the first and second \nround with a few severe thunderstorms possible on Friday, \nespecially along and south of the Route 30 corridor. The \npotential for stronger storms would be a little earlier in the \nday, from late morning through mid-afternoon. \n\nThe cold front has trended slower to clear the local area, thus \nprolonging the potential for showers and thunderstorms. Another \nround is possible across NW Ohio on Friday night and even into \nSaturday now. This should shift south with time on Saturday as we \nfinally see a cap developing with increasing subsidence. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nHigh pressure over the Great Lakes will begin to \nbuild in from the north on Saturday, firmly taking hold of the area \nfor Sunday and Monday. A drier airmass will accompany high pressure \nwith temperatures settling back to seasonal norms in the upper 70s \nto near 80 degrees.",
